# Go-channel IPC (replaces SyncChannel / SMT sync)

Inter-process communication uses Go channels. Do not add shared caches, mutable statics, or other cross-process shortcuts. There is **no Z3 / SMT** on the Go run path.

## Channels and modifiers

- **`service` transition** — hub (old provider). Stays in the **external** alphabet; channel key is the public action name (global). At most one service per action name.
- **`client` transition** — syncs **only** with a matching `service`. Meeting a service removes the client from the external alphabet; clients never sync with each other.
- **Ordinary (untagged)** — when peers meet under `||`, they become **composition-private** (scoped channel key, composition-hidden). Two-static or hub shapes still apply for untagged multi-class offers that remain external.
- **`internal` / `timer`** — occurrence-local keys (not name-global). Keep these; do not replace with service/client.
- A `static proc` appears at most once; only dynamic procs may be spawned via non-`initially` constructors.

## Sender / receiver

- Deduce direction at **compile time** from guard bindings (`=` / `<=>` / `in`) and body reads (`ChannelDirectionPass`).
- Hub / service direction must be uniform; tie → dynamic sends, hub receives.
- **Reply args:** receiver-only bindings that the sender body reads → reply channel.
- Do not reintroduce runtime solver-based rendezvous or SyncChannel session affinity.

## Composition

- `||` lists system members. Ordinary syncs that meet under a compose step get a **scope-private** channel key and leave the external alphabet.
- `service` stays external so further clients can still sync on the same public name.
- No `provider` / `session` modifiers; no `exitSession` / `killSessionPeer`.
